  "song": "kling-gloeckchen",
  "title": "Kling, Glöckchen, klingelingeling",
  "composer": "Traditional",
  "about": "Kling, Glöckchen, klingelingeling means Ring, little bell, ring-a-ling-a-ling. Karl Enslin wrote the words, and they were first printed in 1854. In them the Christ Child begs the children to open the door, because the winter is so cold. The first edition had a tune by Wilhelm Speyer, but nobody knows who wrote the tune sung today.",
  "funFact": "Some books name Benedikt Widmann as the composer, but that is a mix-up. He edited the collection that first printed the words.",
  "mythic": null,
